{"id":"418035b4-92e0-4ef2-bca5-0f3db151b94b","type":"level","createdDate":"2026-06-13T23:10:16.026Z","createdBy":"chaitanya.ponnapati@codeninjas.com","lastModifiedDate":"2026-06-13T23:10:16.026Z","lastModifiedBy":"chaitanya.ponnapati@codeninjas.com","attributes":{"tags":["CREATE"],"title":"Level 1","sequence":1,"activity":{"id":"dc72025e-7633-4020-8bd8-e6d47cb5fcbe","type":"activity","sequence":3,"group":{"id":"dc72025e-7633-4020-8bd8-e6d47cb5fcbe","type":"build","name":null,"sequence":8,"subgroups":[{"id":"dc72025e-7633-4020-8bd8-e6d47cb5fcbe","type":"subgroup","sequence":1,"name":"build 1","steps":[{"id":"5986e327-a1cb-42c1-941e-42e84d2bdd36","hints":"<p>❓Did you type a number between 1-15 to set the color parameter in the parentheses in <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">scene.setBackgroundColor(color)</strong>?</p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" height=\"27\" width=\"344\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ How do you change the color the background is set to?&nbsp;</p><p>❓ How can you test out the background color code to see what color appears on the screen?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡 Remind the ninja that the value inside the parentheses of the&nbsp;<strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">scene.setBackgroundColor(color)</strong> code is an <strong>argument</strong>.</p><p>💡 Remind the ninja that each color is assigned to a number based on its order in the color palette. Encourage the ninja to use different numbers in the parameter and play the project to see what color appears. They can also use the color chart included in the instructions to help them select a color to use. <span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Additionally, the Ninja can also open the paint editor via the </span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">sprites.create </strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">code and hover over each color option to view the corresponding color.</span></p><p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\"></span><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/b51kdxqe.3z5.png\"></p>","instructions":"<p>Begin the project by adding a background color!</p><p><br></p><p>Open <strong>Scene</strong>, then drag out <strong>[set background color to (color)]</strong> into the editor. Notice how the code block turns into JavaScript text:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">scene.setBackgroundColor(0)\n</pre><p>Inside the parentheses, replace <span class=\"ql-font-monospace\">0</span> with a number between 1-15 to set the background color. Try out different values or use the color chart to select a color for the background.</p><p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/151wagvj.ypy.png\"></p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶<strong><em> </em></strong>to see the background color appear on the screen!</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"e46d0c52-b56c-4182-b7bc-b5d5717dfdaf","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">❓Did you add, name, and select an image for 2 different sprites?</span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/twq4d0qf.u2m.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ What might be good names for the two sprites in order to tell them apart?&nbsp;</p><p>❓ Why might it be important to separate the code for the two different sprites? How is this similar to blocks?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡 Remind the ninja that because they are coding in text, they will need to manually replace <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite</strong><strong> </strong>by typing the name of their sprite in each code block. For this reason, the sprite names should be short and descriptive. Just as in blocks, sprite should be written in camelCase—using capital letters to indicate where spaces should be.</p><p>💡 Point out to the ninja that just like in blocks where they use different functions and containers to separate and organize code, they can do the same in text by manually adding spaces between chunks of code.</p>","instructions":"<p>Add 2 different sprites to the project!</p><p><br></p><p>Open <strong>Sprites</strong> and drag <strong>[sprite (img) of kind (kind)]</strong> onto a blank line under the existing code. Notice how the code block turns into JavaScript text:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">let mySprite = sprites.create(img`...`, SpriteKind.Player)\n</pre><p><br></p><p>Then, create 5 blank lines of code by pressing <strong>Enter </strong>on the keyboard 5 times. Open <strong>Sprites</strong> again and drag <strong>[sprite (img) of kind (kind)]</strong> onto the last blank line.&nbsp;</p><p>Click the palette <img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" height=\"26\" width=\"27\">to the left of the code to select a sprite. In the editor, highlight <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite</strong><strong> </strong>and replace it by typing a new name using camelCase. Repeat for the second sprite.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶<strong><em> </em></strong>to see two sprites appear on the screen!</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"5c9fda2c-cb0f-4627-8ca6-2cedd1c65571","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">❓ Did you set each sprite’s position to different x and y coordinates? </span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/lg5pfky2.dxs.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ Where should each <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite.setPosition(0,0)</strong> be placed so that each sprite is set to the correct position?</p><p>❓ Which <strong>argument </strong>sets the sprite’s x coordinate? Which <strong>argument </strong>sets the sprite’s&nbsp;y coordinate?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡 Guide the ninja in dragging the code block into the JavaScript editor, so that it appears on the correct line. They can drag the block to the end of the line of code before it, so that it appears directly below.</p><p>💡 Point out to the ninja that the visual coordinate grid does not exist in the JavaScript editor to help them map out where to place a sprite. Remind the ninja that the x coordinate value range is 0-160, and the y coordinate value range is 0-120.</p>","instructions":"<p>Make the sprites appear in different positions on the screen!</p><p><br></p><p>Open <strong>Sprites </strong>and drag <strong>[set (mySprite) position to x y]</strong> underneath the code that creates the first sprite. Notice how the code block turns into JavaScript text:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">mySprite.setPosition(0, 0)\n</pre><p>Replace <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite</strong><strong> </strong>with the name of the first sprite. In the parentheses, change the first <strong>argument</strong> (<strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">0</strong>) to a number between 0-160 to set the <strong>x </strong>value, then change the second <strong>argument</strong> (<strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">0</strong>)&nbsp;to a number between 0-120 to set the <strong>y </strong>value.</p><p><br></p><p>Set the second sprite’s position by repeating the steps above, dragging the code underneath the code that creates the second sprite.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶<strong><em> </em></strong>to see the 2 sprites appear in different positions on screen!</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"54aea842-c212-4ca1-aa7f-0bfbc58c597d","hints":"<p>❓ Did you replace <strong>mySprite </strong>with the name of the first sprite in <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">controller.moveSprite(sprite)</strong>?</p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/ks5g5q0x.j3m.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ Why might the default <strong>argument </strong>in <strong>controller.moveSprite(mySprite)</strong> be set to mySprite?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡 In <strong>controller.moveSprite(mySprite)</strong>, the default <strong>argument </strong>is set to <strong>mySprite </strong>because it is not set to a specific sprite. Remind the ninja that they must replace <strong>mySprite </strong>with the sprite name to control a specific sprite’s movement.</p>","instructions":"<p>Code the first sprite to move with the direction buttons!</p><p><br></p><p>Open <strong>Controller</strong> and drag <strong>[move (mySprite) with buttons]</strong> underneath the code that sets the first sprite’s position. Notice how the code block turns into JavaScript text:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">controller.moveSprite(mySprite)\n</pre><p><br></p><p>The word <strong>mySprite </strong>is used as a placeholder until an <strong>argument</strong> is added. Inside the parentheses, replace <strong>mySprite </strong>by typing the name of the first sprite.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶<strong><em> </em></strong>to use the direction buttons to move the first sprite around the screen!</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"817396c2-cbbf-4d1a-bc4a-c7fa12970efc","hints":"<p>❓ Did you replace <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite</strong><strong> </strong>with the first sprite’s name in <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite.setStayInScreen(on)</strong>?</p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/oqop1uof.nqs.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ Which sprite should be coded to stay in screen? Where should the code be placed?</p><p>❓ Why is the default parameter in <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite.setStayInScreen(true)</strong> set to <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">true</strong>? What does this look like in blocks?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡 Explain to the ninjas that just like in blocks, each code block has a different <strong>argument </strong>for its default. In the <strong>[set sprite stay in screen]</strong> block, there is a toggle that can be used to set the effect &lt;ON&gt; or &lt;OFF&gt;. This appears as a Boolean in text, where <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">true</strong><strong> </strong>means the effect is turned on and <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">false</strong><strong> </strong>means the effect is turned off.</p>","instructions":"<p>Oh no! The sprite disappeared when it moved outside of the screen! Code the sprite to stay in screen.</p><p><br></p><p>Open <strong>Sprites</strong> and drag <strong>[set (mySprite) stay in screen (true)]</strong> underneath the code that moves the first sprite with buttons. Notice how the code block turns into JavaScript text:</p><p><br></p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">mySprite.setStayInScreen(true)\n</pre><p><br></p><p>Replace <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">mySprite</strong><strong> </strong>with the first sprite’s name. Since <span class=\"ql-font-monospace\">true</span> is the default <strong>argument</strong>,&nbsp;the <strong>[stay in screen]</strong> effect will be turned <em>on</em> when the project begins.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶<strong><em> </em></strong>to move the first sprite around the screen and notice how it stays in screen.</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"8eebc94f-9b4c-40df-8195-3cfa7a1557e1","hints":"<p>❓ Did you replace <strong>myEnemy </strong>and <strong>mySprite </strong>in <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">myEnemy.follow(target, speed)</strong>?</p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/3eu4nyow.k3z.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ What should <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">myEnemy</strong><strong> </strong>be replaced with? What should <strong>mySprite </strong>be replaced with? Why?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡 Remind the ninja of the importance of sequence! In the <strong>myEnemy.follow(mySprite)</strong> code, sprite names need to be typed in the correct places so that the desired sprite follows the other sprite. Additionally, this code needs to be placed after the sprites are already set, so that the project is able to run without errors.</p>","instructions":"<p>Code&nbsp;the second sprite to follow the first sprite!</p><p><br></p><p>Open <strong>Sprites</strong> and drag <strong>[set (myEnemy) follow (mySprite)] </strong>underneath the code that sets the second sprite’s position. Notice how the code block turns into JavaScript text:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">myEnemy.follow(mySprite)\n</pre><p><br></p><p>Replace <strong class=\"ql-font-monospace\">myEnemy</strong><strong> </strong>with the second sprite’s name. Then, replace <strong>mySprite </strong>in the parentheses with the first sprite’s name.</p><p><br></p><p>In the parentheses, add a speed parameter by typing a comma ,&nbsp;then a value between 0-100, to set how fast the second sprite should follow the first sprite.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶<strong><em> </em></strong>to see the second sprite follow the first sprite around the screen! Use the direction buttons to move the first sprite away from the second sprite.</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"7eaedfb6-8883-41b2-be8f-503c5cc480cb","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">❓ Did you switch back to the blocks editor? What looks similar and what looks different?</span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/2loiomju.1cs.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓ Does the code work as expected in blocks?</p><p>❓ When coding in text, how do the parameters appear differently than in blocks?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:</p><p>💡 Help the ninja make connections between the text code they have just written and their knowledge of blocks. Ninjas should be very familiar with these concepts already, so encourage them to think about the similarities and differences between blocks and text.</p><p><br></p>","instructions":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Test out the project. Does it work as expected?</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Look over the </span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">syntax </strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">of the code in this project: the capitalization of certain letters, the spacing, and the symbols - commas, parentheses, and quotation marks. Notice that changing the syntax might make the project unable to run.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Switch to the blocks editor to see what the JavaScript code looks like in blocks! Notice what is similar about the code, and what looks different: how are sprite names and other parameters formatted in blocks, compared to JavaScript?</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" height=\"53\" width=\"294\"></span></p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null}],"title":"Follow Me!","project":{"editor":{"filters":[{"name":"controll